---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildFAQSchema } from "../../lib/seo";

const pageFaqs = [
  {
    question: "What usually stops students from reaching Band 7 in IELTS writing?",
    answer:
      "The most common reasons are weak paragraph development, unclear position, repetitive vocabulary, and grammar mistakes that appear when students try to sound too advanced.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do I need very advanced vocabulary for Band 7?",
    answer:
      "No. You need precise vocabulary, not rare vocabulary. Clear, natural language is better than memorized words that do not fit the sentence well.",
  },
  {
    question: "How can I move from Band 6 to Band 7 faster?",
    answer:
      "Use a repeatable structure, write fewer but better practice essays, and review the same errors until they stop happening.",
  },
];

const pageStructuredData = [
  buildBreadcrumbSchema(Astro.site, [
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", path: "/blog/" },
    { name: "How to Get Band 7 in IELTS Writing", path: "/blog/band-7-writing-strategy/" },
  ]),
  buildFAQSchema(pageFaqs),
];
---

<Layout
  title="How to Get Band 7 in IELTS Writing | Clear Strategy That Works"
  description="A practical Band 7 IELTS writing plan with stronger paragraphing, better grammar control, and study habits that help students move up from Band 6."
  type="article"
  structuredData={pageStructuredData}
>
  <article class="section guide-article">
    <a class="guide-back-link" href="/blog">Back to blog</a>

    <header class="guide-header">
      <span class="guide-tag">IELTS writing guide</span>
      <h1 style="margin: 0;">How to get Band 7 in IELTS Writing</h1>
      <div class="guide-meta">
        <span>March 17, 2026</span>
        <span>8 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="guide-lead">
      <p>
        Many students stay at Band 6 because the writing is not weak everywhere. It is weak
        in a few important places: the answer is not direct enough, the body paragraphs are
        too thin, or the grammar becomes less accurate when the student tries to sound more
        advanced.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal is not to make your essay look complicated. The goal is to make it clear,
        organized, and controlled from the first sentence to the last.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-grid guide-grid-2">
      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>What Band 7 usually does better</h2>
        <ul class="guide-list">
          <li>The position is clear early in the essay.</li>
          <li>Each body paragraph has one main idea and supports it properly.</li>
          <li>Grammar shows some range without falling apart.</li>
          <li>Vocabulary is natural and specific, not forced.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="guide-card">
        <h2>What keeps students at Band 6</h2>
        <ul class="guide-list">
          <li>Ideas are listed, but not developed.</li>
          <li>The introduction sounds generic and does not show a strong position.</li>
          <li>The same simple sentence pattern repeats too often.</li>
          <li>Practice is not reviewed carefully enough.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>The four habits that matter most</h2>
      <div class="guide-steps">
        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">1</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Plan for two minutes</h3>
            <p>
              Decide your position and your two main body ideas before you start writing.
              This prevents weak paragraphs and random examples.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">2</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Keep one clear job per paragraph</h3>
            <p>
              A good body paragraph does one thing well: it introduces one reason, explains
              it, and supports it with one useful example.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">3</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Use grammar you can control</h3>
            <p>
              Mix simple and complex sentences, but do not force difficult patterns if they
              create mistakes. Accuracy still matters at Band 7.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="guide-step">
          <span class="guide-step__number">4</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Rewrite after feedback</h3>
            <p>
              One corrected essay teaches more than three unchecked essays. Rewrite the same
              response after you know what went wrong.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>How to sound stronger without sounding fake</h2>
      <div class="guide-example">
        <p><strong>Too simple:</strong> Technology is bad for families because people use phones a lot.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Better:</strong> Technology can weaken family interaction when people spend
          more time on their phones than on face-to-face conversation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="guide-note" style="margin-top: var(--s-3);">
        <p>
          Notice the difference. The second sentence is not full of difficult words. It is
          just more precise and better organized.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>Common Band 6 mistakes</h2>
      <table class="guide-mistake-table">
        <tr>
          <th>Weak move</th>
          <th>Better move</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Writing a vague opinion in the introduction.</td>
          <td>State a direct position and preview the main reasons.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Putting two or three unrelated ideas in one paragraph.</td>
          <td>Give each paragraph one main reason only.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Using advanced words incorrectly.</td>
          <td>Choose simpler words that fit the meaning exactly.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Practicing many essays without reviewing them well.</td>
          <td>Write less, review more, and rewrite the corrected essay.</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>A simple study plan for the next 7 days</h2>
      <ol class="guide-list">
        <li>Write one Task 2 essay using a clear four-paragraph structure.</li>
        <li>Check whether each body paragraph has one main reason and one relevant example.</li>
        <li>Underline grammar mistakes that repeat.</li>
        <li>Rewrite the essay with those mistakes fixed.</li>
        <li>Only then write a second essay.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>Next step</h2>
      <div class="guide-links">
        <a href="/blog/ielts-writing-task-2-structure">
          <strong>Read the Task 2 structure guide</strong>
          <span>Use a stable essay shape before you worry about higher-level language.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/essay-correction">
          <strong>Use essay correction</strong>
          <span>Get direct feedback on the exact habits that keep your score down.</span>
        </a>
        <a href="/lessons/ielts/writing">
          <strong>Open IELTS writing lessons</strong>
          <span>Study the grammar and vocabulary patterns that support stronger essays.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="guide-card" style="margin-top: var(--s-4);">
      <h2>FAQ</h2>
      <div class="guide-faq">
        {pageFaqs.map((item) => (
          <details class="card">
            <summary>{item.question}</summary>
            <p class="muted" style="margin-top: var(--s-2); margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
